13:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1b",null,{"guide":{"date":"June 27, 1864","place":"South of Kennesaw Mountain, Georgia","result":"Newton’s division was repulsed with more than 650 casualties; Cleburne’s division held its works","facts":[{"label":"Main U.S. formation","value":"Newton’s division, IV Corps, Army of the Cumberland"},{"label":"Confederate defenders","value":"Cleburne’s division, Hardee’s corps"},{"label":"Wagner and Kimball losses","value":"About 400 casualties"},{"label":"Newton’s division losses","value":"More than 650 casualties"},{"label":"Cleburne’s reported losses","value":"About a dozen casualties"},{"label":"Senior U.S. loss","value":"Brigadier General Charles G. Harker killed"}],"maps":[{"title":"Troop movements at Kennesaw Mountain","caption":"The National Park Service map shows Newton’s attack in blue against Cleburne’s line in red, north of Thomas’s main assault at Cheatham Hill.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/orgs/1539/the-southeast-chronicles-kennesaw-mountain-national-battlefield-park.htm","image":"/images/kennesaw-troop-movements.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of troop movements at Kennesaw Mountain on June 27, 1864"},{"title":"Confederate positions across Kennesaw Mountain","caption":"This 1864 drawing shows the Confederate artillery positions across Pigeon Hill, Little Kennesaw, and Big Kennesaw. Newton attacked south of the heights.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660525/","image":"/images/kennesaw-battlefield-drawing.jpg","alt":"1864 drawing of Confederate artillery positions across Kennesaw Mountain"},{"title":"The battlefield today","caption":"Newton’s division attacked the Confederate line between the Dallas Road and Cheatham Hill sectors, south of Kennesaw Mountain.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-84.63%2C33.93%2C-84.57%2C33.99&layer=mapnik&marker=33.956%2C-84.596"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:27","audioTime":87,"title":"Sherman assigned each army a role","detail":"Schofield was to press the Confederate left, McPherson was to attack near Pigeon Hill and demonstrate against the mountain, and Thomas was to make the main assault."},{"time":"2:40","audioTime":160,"title":"Newton prepared to attack Cleburne","detail":"John Newton’s IV Corps division faced Patrick Cleburne’s entrenched Confederate division south of Kennesaw Mountain."},{"time":"3:51","audioTime":231,"title":"Wagner’s brigade started around 9 a.m.","detail":"George D. Wagner’s five regiments advanced about an hour after the appointed time and immediately received artillery and musket fire."},{"time":"5:18","audioTime":318,"title":"The assault reached the Confederate obstacles","detail":"Some soldiers reached the stakes and abatis but could not clear them under close-range fire. Wagner’s men made two attempts."},{"time":"6:00","audioTime":360,"title":"Kimball moved up in support","detail":"Nathan Kimball’s brigade advanced on Wagner’s left shortly after 9 a.m. Rough ground and Confederate fire also stopped this brigade."},{"time":"8:51","audioTime":531,"title":"Newton ordered a withdrawal","detail":"By 10 a.m., Wagner and Kimball were pinned down and unable to enter the works. Confederate soldiers fired heavily as the U.S. troops fell back."},{"time":"10:14","audioTime":614,"title":"Wagner and Kimball lost about 400 men","detail":"The two brigades failed to penetrate Cleburne’s line and withdrew after less than two hours of fighting."},{"time":"10:34","audioTime":634,"title":"Harker’s brigade attacked farther south","detail":"Charles G. Harker led eight regiments against the junction of Cleburne’s and Cheatham’s divisions."},{"time":"13:19","audioTime":799,"title":"Opdyke led the skirmish line","detail":"Emerson Opdyke and the 125th Ohio formed part of a strong skirmish screen ordered to clear the front and enter the Confederate works if possible."},{"time":"14:02","audioTime":842,"title":"Harker advanced around 9 a.m.","detail":"The skirmishers drove in Confederate pickets but stopped at the abatis and stakes under heavy fire. The brigade crowded behind them."},{"time":"15:01","audioTime":901,"title":"U.S. flags briefly reached the works","detail":"Accounts place the flags of the 51st and 27th Illinois on the parapet. Too few men reached the line to hold a position there."},{"time":"15:44","audioTime":944,"title":"Harker was shot near the works","detail":"Harker rode to within about 15 yards of the Confederate line. A bullet passed through his right arm and entered his body."},{"time":"16:17","audioTime":977,"title":"Luther Bradley took command","detail":"Bradley concluded the brigade could not remain under the works and ordered it back. Confederate fire caused additional losses during the retreat."},{"time":"19:01","audioTime":1141,"title":"Crossfire struck Harker’s brigade","detail":"Harker attacked where Lucius Polk’s and Alfred Vaughan’s brigades joined at an angle. Fire from both directions hit the U.S. formation."},{"time":"20:15","audioTime":1215,"title":"A Confederate soldier captured the 27th Illinois flag","detail":"A sergeant in the 29th Tennessee took the colors during the close fighting at the works."},{"time":"20:26","audioTime":1226,"title":"Gunfire ignited dry vegetation","detail":"Muzzle blast and burning debris set fires in front of the Confederate line, trapping wounded U.S. soldiers near the works."},{"time":"21:52","audioTime":1312,"title":"A temporary truce allowed wounded men to escape","detail":"Colonel William Martin displayed a white handkerchief and firing stopped locally. Soldiers from both sides helped remove U.S. wounded from the fire."},{"time":"24:01","audioTime":1441,"title":"Newton’s division lost more than 650 men","detail":"The episode contrasts the U.S. loss with Johnston’s claim that Cleburne’s division suffered only about a dozen casualties."},{"time":"25:02","audioTime":1502,"title":"Harker died at the field hospital","detail":"Harker died around 1 p.m. with David Stanley and John Newton present. He had received his brigadier general’s commission only 20 days earlier."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"blue","branches":[{"commander":"George H. Thomas","command":"Army of the Cumberland","subordinates":"Oliver O. Howard’s IV Corps"},{"commander":"Oliver O. Howard","command":"IV Corps","subordinates":"John Newton’s division"},{"commander":"John Newton","command":"2nd Division, IV Corps","subordinates":"George Wagner, Nathan Kimball, and Charles Harker"},{"commander":"George D. Wagner","command":"2nd Brigade","subordinates":"Five regiments from Indiana, Ohio, and Kentucky"},{"commander":"Nathan Kimball","command":"1st Brigade","subordinates":"Regiments from Illinois, Wisconsin, and Missouri"},{"commander":"Charles G. Harker","command":"3rd Brigade","subordinates":"Eight regiments from Illinois, Ohio, and Kentucky"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"red","branches":[{"commander":"William J. Hardee","command":"Hardee’s Corps","subordinates":"Patrick Cleburne’s and Benjamin Cheatham’s divisions"},{"commander":"Patrick R. Cleburne","command":"Cleburne’s Division","subordinates":"Held the line attacked by Wagner and Kimball"},{"commander":"Lucius E. Polk","command":"Brigade, Cleburne’s Division","subordinates":"Held one side of the angle struck by Harker"},{"commander":"Alfred J. Vaughan","command":"Brigade, Cheatham’s Division","subordinates":"Held the other side of the angle struck by Harker"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Charles G. Harker","role":"Led the southern brigade of Newton’s assault and was killed","side":"United States"},{"name":"John Newton","role":"Commanded the IV Corps division making the attack","side":"United States"},{"name":"George D. Wagner","role":"Led the central brigade against Cleburne’s works","side":"United States"},{"name":"Nathan Kimball","role":"Led the supporting brigade on Wagner’s left","side":"United States"},{"name":"Emerson Opdyke","role":"Led the strong skirmish line ahead of Harker’s brigade","side":"United States"},{"name":"Luther P. Bradley","role":"Assumed command of Harker’s brigade and ordered its withdrawal","side":"United States"},{"name":"Patrick R. Cleburne","role":"Commanded the entrenched Confederate division that stopped most of Newton’s attack","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"William Martin","role":"Arkansas colonel who initiated a temporary truce to remove wounded soldiers from the fire","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[{"name":"Charles G. Harker","side":"United States","role":"Brigadier general commanding the 3rd Brigade, Newton’s division","outcome":"Mortally wounded near the Confederate works and died at about 1 p.m. on June 27"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/kennesaw-troop-movements.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map showing U.S. attacks and the Confederate line at Kennesaw Mountain","caption":"Newton’s division attacked Cleburne’s line north of the main U.S. assault at Cheatham Hill.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/orgs/1539/the-southeast-chronicles-kennesaw-mountain-national-battlefield-park.htm"},{"image":"/images/kennesaw-battlefield-drawing.jpg","alt":"Theodore R. Davis drawing of Confederate artillery positions at Kennesaw Mountain","caption":"A wartime drawing of the Confederate position across Kennesaw Mountain.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660525/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Episode 521 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2026/02/23/civil-war-podcast-episode-521/"},{"label":"National Park Service battle summary","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=ga015"},{"label":"National Park Service troop movements map","url":"https://www.nps.gov/orgs/1539/the-southeast-chronicles-kennesaw-mountain-national-battlefield-park.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service Union order of battle","url":"https://www.nps.gov/kemo/learn/historyculture/union-order-of-battle.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service Confederate order of battle","url":"https://www.nps.gov/kemo/learn/historyculture/confederate-order-of-battle.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress Kennesaw battlefield drawing","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660525/"}]}}]}]
